    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So it's more about acknowledging the limits of the data. I've been reading some older texts that talk about 'noise' in data as if it's almost like something acting on the samples to distort them. If I were to describe how these random errors or stochastic fluctuations actually manifest during the data collection process, how would you suggest I describe that 'interference' without making it sound like the data itself is unreliable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When describing how random errors manifest, you should shift the language from \"interference\" to \"variability\" or \"stochastic fluctuation.\" You can explain that these fluctuations arise from the natural heterogeneity of the population you are studying—meaning not every individual behaves exactly the same way, even if they belong to the same group. You might describe it as the inherent, unpredictable variation between individuals or observations, which is a feature of the real world, not necessarily a flaw in your measurement tools. This keeps the focus on the nature of the phenomenon itself, rather than suggesting your data collection was flawed.",
      "source": "target",
